# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the WallpaperX daemon.
#
# Everything here is a *third-party* library TSan cannot see into, not a WallpaperX
# race. Nothing in backend/ or renderer/ belongs in this file -- if a report names our
# own code, it is a finding, not noise. That rule survives intact; read on before adding
# an exception to it.
#
# THE LOADER ARTIFACT IS HANDLED BY A FLAG, NOT BY AN ENTRY HERE. `vkDestroyDevice` and
# `vkDestroyInstance` unlock a loader-internal mutex TSan never saw locked, and because
# the loader is uninstrumented TSan attributes the unlock to the nearest instrumented
# frame -- `Device::destroyResources()`, ours. `called_from_lib:` cannot catch it for
# exactly that reason. llvmpipe's LLVM thread pool produces the same shape through
# `std::condition_variable::wait`, attributed to libstdc++. Both are mutex-*misuse*
# reports, not races, so `report_mutex_bugs=0` (set on the TSan tests by
# backend/CMakeLists.txt) removes the whole class without naming a single one of our
# functions. Measured 2026-08-04: with it, data races between instrumented threads, the
# condition_variable use-after-destroy, and a race placed inside `destroyResources`
# itself are all still reported.
#
# What that costs, stated plainly: TSan no longer reports double-lock, unlock-of-unlocked
# or destroy-while-locked anywhere. Data race detection -- the reason this suite runs TSan
# at all -- is untouched.
#
# WHAT NOT TO REACH FOR: `ignore_noninstrumented_modules=1` looks like the purpose-built
# answer here, and it does silence the loader artifact. Do not use it. Measured
# 2026-08-04: it also silences a use-after-destroy on a `std::condition_variable`
# notified by a live thread -- 2 reports without it, 0 with -- because the notify path
# runs through libstdc++/libc. That is precisely the shape of the `NameWatcher` bug this
# suite found, so the flag blinds the gate to the bug class it exists to catch. It leaves
# plain races between instrumented threads visible, which is what makes it so convincing
# and so dangerous.

# Most entries here match nothing on this machine; they cover hardware and configurations
# this tier does not run. Do not delete one for matching zero. To re-measure, add
# `print_suppressions=1` -- and keep the ICD pinned to Lavapipe, or the run selects NVIDIA
# and the sanitizer dies on a SEGV before printing, which reads as "nothing matched".
# Counts are in PLAN.md.

# The Khronos validation layer. Uninstrumented, and it keeps its own concurrent state
# maps behind rwlocks TSan cannot pair up. Present only because the tests ask for
# validation -- the shipped daemon does not load it at all.
called_from_lib:libVkLayer_khronos_validation.so

# Mesa / Lavapipe. Uninstrumented, and llvmpipe's rasteriser uses its own worker pool
# with atomics and futexes TSan reads as unsynchronised.
#
# Still the right object despite Mesa's shared libgallium: this one neither links nor
# dlopens it, so the Lavapipe driver is here.
called_from_lib:libvulkan_lvp.so
called_from_lib:libvulkan.so
called_from_lib:libLLVM
# Mesa's shared gallium object, which its EGL and Vulkan front ends both dlopen. It
# re-initialises a pthread_barrier_t on the caller's stack while a shader thread it
# started is still destroying it -- both accesses inside Mesa, ours only below them on the
# stack.
#
# Narrow, like Qt below and unlike the three entries above: `race:` matches the report's
# own frames, so a race between this project's threads is still reported while Mesa is
# loaded and running. Measured 2026-09-08 -- a deliberate race between frame_import_probe's
# main thread and one it starts is still reported with this entry active. What it does cost:
# a report is hidden whenever a libgallium frame appears in any stack the report prints,
# which includes the creating stack of a thread Mesa started.
#
# No version in the pattern, deliberately: the object is named libgallium-26.2.2-arch1.1.so
# here, matching is substring, and an entry carrying the version would stop matching on the
# next Mesa package and turn this gate red for a change nobody made. Measured the same day:
# `race:libgallium-27` does not match and the race comes back, which is what a versioned
# entry would do after an upgrade.
race:libgallium

# The Vulkan loader spawns nothing itself but dlopen's ICDs whose init TSan attributes
# to whichever thread got there first.
called_from_lib:libvulkan_radeon.so
called_from_lib:libvulkan_intel.so

# ffmpeg's frame threads order their copies, fills and frees with C11 atomics, which the
# uninstrumented library hides, so its own memcpy/memset/malloc/free on decoder state read as
# races. Verified against an ffmpeg built with the sanitizer: none of those reports appear.
# A race between two of this project's threads stays visible (a planted one is still reported);
# what is lost is a race between our code and one of those ffmpeg calls on the same memory.
called_from_lib:libavcodec.so
called_from_lib:libavutil.so

# sd-bus runs the connection's event loop; its internal reference counting is
# uninstrumented and shows up on every signal emission from the render thread.
called_from_lib:libsystemd.so

# Dormant: the only test these three cover, `qml_states`, is `no-tsan` and disabled in the
# sanitizer tier. Kept for when that label is lifted.
#
# Qt, for the plugin's headless state tests. Uninstrumented: the QML engine runs a
# QQmlThread and the reply path starts a QNetworkAccessManager thread, and both are torn
# down by Qt at exit in ways TSan cannot pair with the main thread. Measured on Qt 6.11.2 --
# every frame of all five reports is libQt6Core, libc or libtsan itself, and not one names
# this project.
#
# Narrow on purpose. `race:` matches the report's own location rather than any stack that
# passes through Qt, so a race between our own instrumented threads is still reported even
# while it is running inside the QML engine. That is the distinction the whole file rests
# on, and it is why this is not `called_from_lib:libQt6Core.so`.
race:libQt6Core.so
# glib, which is Qt's event-loop backend here: the eventfd its main context uses to wake
# the network thread is written on that thread and read on ours, with the pairing inside
# uninstrumented glib. Same shape, same evidence -- no frame in this project.
race:libglib-2.0.so
# This project starts no QThread -- the daemon uses std::thread throughout -- so this can
# only ever match a thread Qt started for itself.
thread:QThread::start
